Z||000002||Fie Ro||10-16-2001||05:03 AM||||How can I measure that?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fie Ro||10-18-2001||05:56 AM||||Yesterday I checked the wires and with some help I checked ignition and timing and I DO have spark.<BR>So it must be some kind of fuel-delivery problem...I was thinking about the fuel pump but it was replaced last year ("they" say it is new). maybe it's a leaking (cold start) injector??? It can't be one of the normal injectors because then they should all be bad (no sign of combustion in any cilinder while trying to start) fuelpump fuse is ok...what can I check without a fuelpressure meter? maybe I have a bad fuelpressure regulator, do they go bad?<P>I need some help on this...thx<P>------------------<BR>-The Beauty & The Beast-<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero GT & <BR>1977 Dodge W200||||reg||8
Z||000004||Oreif||10-18-2001||09:08 AM||||Fuel pressure regulators can go bad. Fuel pump may be low in pressure. Fuel Filter could be bad/clogged. A clogged fuel filter will also lower your fuel pressure at the injectors. The used coil could be "weak". <BR>Just a few quick thoughts on what it could be.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fie Ro||10-18-2001||10:04 AM||||Fuelfilter was replaced last year but I can recall that the one that came out was very very dirty, maybe there's still a lot of dirt in the system (maybe pump died because of that)....I will check that. I will also look for a pressuremeter, makes it easier to diagnose (I hope) I'll also try my spare ECM to check if that makes a difference.....trying the easy things first! Thanks so far...<P>------------------<BR>-The Beauty & The Beast-<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero GT & <BR>1977 Dodge W200||||reg||9
Z||000006||Fie Ro||10-22-2001||12:18 PM||||update: I tested the G-terminal on the aldl connector and its getting about 10volts when starting (bit low battery by then, now I borrowed a booster because I think I'll need it!). I can hear the pump running.<BR>Pushed the pin on the Schrader valve and yes there was pressure. Still don't know its the right pressure.<BR>Pulled out a sparkplug and it was wet. All are indications of getting the fuel in the cilinders....or maybe too much??<BR>Also switched the ECM (came from an automatic but should work for starting?) NOPE, put the original back.<P>I WAS getting spark but I'm still thinking about the ignition failing somewhere. When the module is bad will the engine still be able to give some spark? The rest is checked or replaced.The module was replaced also last year but I can't check whether its working right..still looking for a possible bad timing. tried again various positions of the distributor before returning to the marked one....how much tolerance can it have??<P>I don't know where to find the solution, some more HELP PLEASE!!!<P>------------------<BR>-The Beauty & The Beast-<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero GT & <BR>1977 Dodge W200||||reg||9
Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||10-22-2001||12:35 PM||||About your ignition timing.<BR>Have you turned the crank around to #1 TDC on the compression stroke? Where is your rotor pointing to in relation to the distriubtor cap if it was in the installed position? (If different, correct it and attempt to start)<BR>You may have a trashed your timing chain and gear(s)! An easy way to find out is to do a compression check and see if your building any!||||reg||1
Z||000008||87GTZ34||10-22-2001||12:37 PM||||Yes it could be the ignition module.  It is always good to carry a spare so if you don't have one you should pick one up and swap it out.  My experience (and others on PFF) is to only get the GM module due to heat performance.  Another thing to try is to unhook the tach filter as they will short when they get old.  It sounds like you need to pull the plugs and clean/dry them and let some air get to the cylinders to help dry the fuel as well (don't crank it w/o the plugs though as you'll pull dirt into the cyls).  <BR>  If your problem is fuel related it could be bad injectors although they usually don't go that suddenly, or it could be the cold-start injector leaking.  If it is leaking, most likely it is mechanical and unplugging the connector won't help.  Here's where you need to get your hands on a fuel pressure gauge...  Hook-up the gauge, turn key to the on position until the pump cuts off (after approx 2 seconds), then watch the pressure gauge to see if it can hold pressure.  If it falls (somewhat quickly), that doesn't necessarily mean you have leaky injectors, it just means that you need to have them tested.  If the pressure doesn't leak down, then you don't have any fuel systems leaks including the injectors.  Hope this helps!!! GL||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fie Ro||10-22-2001||12:54 PM||||Indiana: I haven't done that yet. But I will to be sure about the timning. BTW If the chain/gears are thrashed, wouldn't you hear it while starting?<BR>87GTZ34: The module is an E-tron from the fierostore, has been in for maybe 5000 miles. Could be broken though. I will unhook the tach filter. I hope I can get a fuel pressure gauge tomorrow. Thanks for your help.<P>first symptomes (when it all started) were like the engine was missing and high fuel consumption so something was already going to be wrong then....||||reg||11
Z||000010||Indiana_resto_guy||10-22-2001||06:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fie Ro:<BR>[B]Indiana: I haven't done that yet. But I will to be sure about the timning. BTW If the chain/gears are thrashed, wouldn't you hear it while starting? B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Lets see what happens after you do the timing reset and a compression check (if the timing dosn't work)<BR>About the gears, no. In most cases you won't hear anything unless they are really, really bad. The chain could be so loose that it jumped a couple of teeth and you will never know it. It just runs badly like in a way that you have already described.<BR>How many miles are showing and how much history do you know about the car?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fie Ro||10-23-2001||05:14 AM||||It has 135K miles on it. I have this one for 2 years now. Got a package of bills from the previous owner, well maintained but I haven't seen anything about a replaced timingchain...Engine was out last year, don't think they checked the gears.||||reg||1
Z||000012||87GTZ34||10-23-2001||08:58 AM||||The weakened top end does sound like timing chain stretch.  If you want to check it (coarsely) next time you have it in the garage here is the procedure.  Rotate the crankshaft until your timing mark is at TDC.  Take out #1 spark plug and insert a new (clean) drinking straw.  Turn the crank backwards (CC) until the timing mark hits the 10 degrees BTDC.  Now observe the drinking straw (may need a helper here) and rotate the crank in the forward (clockwise looking at eng from the front) direction until the straw just barely begins to move.  Note the degree change on the harmonic balancer.  That will be the degree chain slop in your gears and chain.  I just replaced mine 2000 miles ago and took pictures.  I will email the before and after to you.  I would still do the fuel leakdown mentioned before just to eliminate a fuel problem.  GL<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 10-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Indiana_resto_guy||10-23-2001||08:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fie Ro:<BR><B>It has 135K miles on it. I have this one for 2 years now. Got a package of bills from the previous owner, well maintained but I haven't seen anything about a replaced timingchain...Engine was out last year, don't think they checked the gears.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>The procedure posted aabove by 87GTZ34 sounds like a good check. Let us know what you come up with when testing is completed.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Fie Ro||10-25-2001||05:46 AM||||I unplugged the tach filter.<BR>I checked the compression of the trunkside cilinders; they all read approx 105 psi just by cranking the engine.<BR>I could find no timingmarks on the balancer, The car is outside and its hard to get a good sight at the balancer so I did the TDC/rotor approach, removed sparkplug 1, put in a pencil and moved the car in second gear<BR>piston at TDC in cilinder 1 : Rotor points to    (the cap) cilinder 1.<BR>I wasn't able to borrow or even buy a suitable fuelpressure tester. Will those for sale at JcWhitney/Jegs fit our fuelrail Schradervalve? they're about $30.<BR>But I do have pressure when pushing the Schadervalve, even 5 minutes after a start attempt there is a little bit of pressure left.....<BR>A was able to borrow a Hei tester at a local GM dealership (quite nice of them). from the coil I get good sparks, from the sparkplugwires it's a bit harder to tell because at that time battery+booster where almost empty and it was hard to crank the engine. And of course at the coil you have 6times the spark that you have at the wires.....I did see "some" spark though<BR>I should think about getting a new cap if this one wasn't already almost new (and checked) and I tried with another (used but good) cap also.......<BR>Other possibilities:<BR>- I read about a possible faulty coolant temp sensor: Ecm thinks it's its -35 and pumps too much fuel. have no codes. can I bypass the sensor?<BR>- a while ago a had a bad connection to my startermotor, I made a new wire and starter button (In my ashtray!) the fault could have been a bad connection or maybe it is my ignition switch???? maybe I should check what more is running from that switch, fuelpump and ignition are working.....SES is on during start. As far as I know SES is powered by the ECM so ECM must have power too....||||reg||9
Z||000015||Cozmo||10-25-2001||07:09 AM||||I had a similiar problem that drove me NUTS.  Ended up being a plugged fuel reurn line.  The only way I was able to find it was by using a fuel pressure meter.  Yes, the JCW/Jegs unit will hook right up, at least GM was smart enough to use a standard fitting.<BR>You're getting quite an education on Fiero engines, aren't you? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Keep us posted on your progress this is the right place to ask these questions...<P>------------------<BR><B>BobC</B><BR><B>'85 2M6 Targa</B><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/targsig.jpg"> <BR><B>Suncoast Fieros</B>||||reg||1
Z||000016||SKIDMARK||10-25-2001||10:50 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>- "sparkplugs look fine, wires also"<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I would suggest a new set of plugs and wires just for GP. Without being able to run the engine in the dark and wet down the wires with a spray bottle to check them, you can't tell by looking at them. The spark plugs also act different outside the cyl. They may look like they're working fine but when you put them back into the pressurized environment of the cyl, they may not be working at all. <BR>A basic tune up is always a good place to start, and is likely needed anyway. I had a similar problem with my 2M4 and it ended up being the wires. <P>------------------<BR>White '85 4cyl coupe<BR>Black '86 v-6 SE<BR>'81 CB900F Super Sport||||reg||1
Z||000017||Fie Ro||10-25-2001||01:30 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>You're getting quite an education on Fiero engines, aren't you?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeahhhh, and I already thought I knew too much about them!!......and there's always some "Fiero voodoo" involved when the bad things happen. And when you find the cause its always too simple too think about.<BR>What did plug your return line? Residu from the fueltank? I suppose it's the front line from the two lines on the left under the battery?<P>skidmark: I not only looked at the wires but also measured resistance while twisting them, but I will replace them anyway. <P>I'm still trying to find a fuelpressuremeter here otherwise I will order in the US, shipping/importing/taxes etc. will take some time though....I'm glad I got a backupcar for these situations, in fact this is why I have a backupcar! ;-))<P>Well, its all part of The great Fiero Experience...<P>------------------<BR>-The Beauty & The Beast-<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero GT & <BR>1977 Dodge W200||||reg||1
Z||000018||johnt671||10-25-2001||03:14 PM||||This mat sound dumb but a couple of years ago I had a similar mystery.  Cost me over $300 to solve.  Unplug the the tach wire off the coil.  Between me and the shop we tried everything to get the car to start(dist,ecm,etc..) and it was a no go.  The shop pulled the tach wire off the coil and the car started right up.  He offered to find the problem with the tach but $300 in labor was enough for me so I took the car and put an aftermarket tack in and it's been fine since then.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Fie Ro||12-13-2001||07:14 AM||||Well,<BR>I got the car to a garage and I decided to take the plenum of. At first sight the injectors and fuelrail seem ok but when I got the injectors off the rail there was a lot of rubbish inside them ! Lots of rusty particals......I brought them to a remanufacture shop and they said they will be able to clean them, however I think one or more are broken. They charge F70,- (about $ 25/30) per injector to clean.<P>I hope that helps solving the problem.<BR>I will keep this updated.||||reg||1
Z||000020||K2m4||12-13-2001||11:45 AM||||TIMING, TIMING, TIMING. if you removed your distributer and rebuilt it, i dont care how carefull you are when putting it back together you will never, i shouldnt say never but rarely get everything back close enough where it will run. Loosen your distributer hold down bolt and rotate the cap and try to start it. Keep doing that till it starts or gow through and time it going through the Top dead center proceedures. I HAD THESAME EXACT PROBLEM YOU DESCIRIBED TO A TEE HAPPEN TO ME  last week. i mean what you described happened to a tee. Lost ignition turned out it was a bad pick up coil and ignition module. when i removed the distributer there was a noticible catch in it when i spun the rotor so i just went out and purchased a new distributer pitting it back in to the exact deatil of my notes and pictures with my digital camara. It wouldnt start to save my life and i was beating my head off the wall trying. ultimately i replaced EVERY SINGLE IGNITION COMPONENT in the car. I MEAN EVERYTHING. still wouldnt start. So for shits and giggles i turned the distributer cap dont ask me which way i just turned it and it sounded like it wanted to start turned it again this time the car started but sounded funky turned it again and it started up and purred nicely long enough to warm up, stuck the paper clip in and timed it. Ive removed the distibuter a 1000 times in these cars in the numerous fieros ive owned and this is the first time this has happened and this is the first time i had to break out with the timing light to time the pig. A lesson learned!  CHECK YOUR TIMING. ill bet you i prime rib dinner its off. There are not  that many components to your ignition system and if you can hear your fuel pump kick on and see the wetness in your TB i can almost gaurentee you you that thats what your problem is!<BR>Sorry for the caps but reading this thread brought back all the aggravation that i went through in the last 6 days trying to figure out the exact same problem you have going!!<P>Good luck<BR><P>------------------<BR>Kerm<BR>86 GT<BR>86 2M4||||reg||1
